    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-lakes.jpg" alt="Great Lakes" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Great_Lakes,_No_Clouds_(4968915002)_Brighter.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NASA, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Bunyoro, or Bunyoro-Kitara, arose in western Uganda on lands associated in tradition with the Empire of Kitara, a realm remembered as ruled first by the Batembuzi and then by the Bachwezi, semi-divine figures linked to the great earthworks of Bigo bya Mugenyi and other sites probably occupied in the fourteenth and fifteenth centuries. According to tradition the Chwezi withdrew mysteriously, and around 1500 Luo-speaking newcomers from the north, ancestors of the Babito dynasty, took power. The first Mubito king is remembered as Isingoma Mpuga Rukidi, who adopted the regalia and rituals of his predecessors and presented his line as their rightful heir; historians accept a Luo migration and a change of dynasty but regard the details and the date as uncertain. The new kingdom centred on the fertile, well-watered country east of Lake Albert, with access to the salt of Kibiro and to good iron ores. In the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries the Babito kings were the most powerful rulers of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region. Their armies raided far to the south, into Nkore, Rwanda and the lands later dominated by Buganda, and traditions in several neighbouring kingdoms recall Nyoro invasions. Branches of the Babito established dynasties in Kiziba and in some of the small kingdoms of Busoga, extending the reach of Nyoro kingship.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#nile"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nile.jpg" alt="Nile" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#nile">Nile</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Beautiful_nature_along_Nile_River_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Fiktube, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bunyoro combined cultivation and herding across a varied landscape. Farmers grew finger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#millet">millet</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sorghum">sorghum</a> and, increasingly, plantains, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, prized above all other wealth, were kept by herders often identified as Bahuma, although the line between pastoral and farming groups was less rigid than in Nkore. The kingdom's economic strength lay in two resources many neighbours lacked: the salt of Kibiro, produced by women from saline soils on the shore of Lake Albert, and high-quality iron, smelted and forged into hoes and weapons that circulated widely across the region. These goods moved through networks of exchange linking the lake shores, the grasslands and the forests, and Nyoro markets drew traders from Buganda, Busoga and beyond. In the nineteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> became the main export, as traders from Zanzibar arrived by way of Buganda and Karagwe and northern traders based in Khartoum reached the kingdom along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nile">Nile</a>, exchanging cloth, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bead">beads</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#copper">copper</a> and firearms. Religion centred on the spirits of the Chwezi, honoured through the <em>embandwa</em> spirit-possession cult and its mediums, on the ancestors and on the ritual role of the king. The colonial period brought catastrophe: war, the loss of fertile counties, sleeping sickness and cattle disease produced, as the historian Shane Doyle has shown, a long demographic and economic decline that lasted well into the twentieth century.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#lake-victoria"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lake-victoria.jpg" alt="Lake Victoria" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS042-E-263206_-_View_of_Earth.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>For most of its history Bunyoro's power rested on its ability to mobilise large forces through its chiefs, who raised warriors from their territories for raids and campaigns. In the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries these armies carried war deep into Nkore, Rwanda and the Buganda region, although their raids rarely produced lasting conquests. From the eighteenth century the balance turned: Buganda, with more centralised command and war canoes on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a>, took Buddu and other border districts, and the secession of Toro around 1830 deprived Bunyoro of rich pastures and the salt lakes of the south-west. The kingdom's military revival came under Omukama Kabalega (r. 1870–1899). He created a standing army, the <em>abarusura</em>, organised in regiments under commanders loyal to him rather than to the hereditary chiefs, and armed it with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#musket">muskets</a> and rifles obtained by trading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ivory-trade">ivory</a> with Khartoum and Zanzibar merchants. In 1872 he drove off Samuel Baker, who had tried to annex Bunyoro for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#egypt">Egypt</a>, after fighting at Masindi, and in 1876 he reconquered Toro. From late 1893, however, British forces under Colonel Henry Colvile, supported by large Ganda armies, invaded the kingdom. Kabalega fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war for more than five years, joined in 1898 by the deposed Kabaka Mwanga of Buganda, until both were captured in Lango in April 1899.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <em>omukama</em> stood at the centre of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a> that, in Nyoro ideology, had been inherited from the Chwezi. His health was bound to the welfare of the land, and court ritual, regalia such as royal drums and spears, and royal tombs kept by hereditary custodians expressed the continuity of the dynasty. The kingdom was divided into territorial chiefdoms, many held by members of the Babito clan or by powerful hereditary families, who owed the king tribute in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a>, grain, salt and iron, as well as labour and warriors. This structure gave great autonomy to princes and chiefs, and successions were often contested by force among the king's sons, which repeatedly weakened the centre and encouraged breakaways such as Toro. Kabalega tried to overcome these weaknesses by appointing commoners loyal to himself, among them commanders of the <em>abarusura</em>, to chiefships, building a more centralised state in the 1870s and 1880s. After his defeat the British ruled Bunyoro as conquered territory, installing his young son Kitahimbwa and then Andereya Duhaga II, and appointing Ganda chiefs over Nyoro districts, which provoked the Nyangire ('I refuse') revolt of 1907. Only in 1933 did a Bunyoro Agreement give the kingdom a formal status comparable to the other agreement kingdoms of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#milton-obote"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/milton-obote.jpg" alt="Milton Obote" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#milton-obote">Milton Obote</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:President_John_F._Kennedy_with_Prime_Minister_Milton_Obote_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Warren K. Leffler, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bunyoro was the leading heir of the Kitara tradition, the idea of a great ancient kingdom whose legitimacy all the dynasties of the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> sought to share. Its Babito rulers and their branch dynasties carried Nyoro institutions into Toro, Kiziba and parts of Busoga, and the Chwezi spirit cults associated with Kitara spread widely across the region. For two centuries Nyoro raids shaped the history of its neighbours, and Buganda's rise was achieved largely at Bunyoro's expense, a rivalry that became the central axis of the region's politics. Bunyoro's iron and salt were among the most important goods exchanged in pre-colonial Uganda. Kabalega became a national hero in independent Uganda, remembered as the most determined resister of British conquest, and in the 1970s Murchison Falls and its national park were for a time renamed after him. The colonial settlement left a lasting grievance, the 'lost counties' transferred to Buganda in the 1890s; the referendum of 1964 that returned two of them to Bunyoro contributed to the crisis between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#milton-obote">Milton Obote</a> and Buganda that ended with the abolition of the kingdoms. The kingdom also became central to African anthropology through John Beattie's studies of the Nyoro state, and the discovery of oil around Lake Albert has revived debate over land and development in the region.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#frederick-lugard"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-lugard.jpg" alt="Frederick Lugard" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LordLugard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elliott &amp;amp; Fry, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Bunyoro's long decline began in the eighteenth century, when Buganda seized its south-eastern districts and dynastic quarrels weakened the centre. Around 1830 Kaboyo Olimi, a son of Omukama Nyamutukura Kyebambe III, broke away to found the Kingdom of Toro, and the mid-nineteenth-century reign of Kamurasi was troubled by civil war among royal claimants. Kabalega's revival restored Bunyoro as a regional power, but it set him against both Buganda and the British, who saw the Nyoro king as an obstacle to their control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nile">Nile</a> and the lakes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#frederick-lugard">Frederick Lugard</a> restored Toro under Kasagama in 1891 and stationed troops along the border, and the campaign launched in 1893–94 occupied the kingdom. As a reward to their Ganda allies, the British transferred large parts of Bunyoro, including sites sacred to the dynasty, to Buganda, creating the 'lost counties'. After his capture in 1899 Kabalega was exiled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#seychelles">Seychelles</a>; he was allowed to return in 1923 but died at Jinja before reaching home. Under colonial rule Bunyoro suffered population loss, economic neglect and the imposition of Ganda chiefs. The 1964 referendum returned Buyaga and Bugangaizi, but the republican constitution of 1967 abolished the monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> Winyi IV. It was restored in 1993 as a cultural institution, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#solomon">Solomon</a> Gafabusa Iguru I was crowned in 1994.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Bunyoro, heir to the Kitara tradition, dominated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-lakes">Great Lakes</a> region in the sixteenth and seventeenth centuries through its armies, salt and iron, lost ground to Buganda, revived under Kabalega and became the emblem of resistance to British conquest before its abolition in 1967 and restoration as a cultural kingdom.</em></p>

